if Date column fails to load "MM-DD-YYYY"
then check the following process
You're trying to create a text date in MM-DD-YYYY format and then convert it into a real Date in Power Query — but you're seeing [DataFormat.Error] when parsing the text into a Date.

Let’s fix it step-by-step, properly and cleanly. 🔧

✅ Step 1: Build a Text Date Column (MM-DD-YYYY)
Go to "Add Column" → "Custom Column", and paste this code:

powerquery

Text.PadStart(Text.From([Month]), 2, "0") & "-" &
Text.PadStart(Text.From([Day]), 2, "0") & "-" &
Text.From([Year])
This will create a text column, say TextDate, in the format: MM-DD-YYYY.

✅ Step 2: Convert the TextDate column to Date Format
Click on the "ABC123" icon on the column header.
Choose Data Type → Date.
If Power Query still gives you [DataFormat.Error], it means your system locale is not recognizing MM-DD-YYYY format.
✅ Step 3 (Best Fix): Use Date.FromText with US Culture explicitly
Instead of changing column type manually, use this code in a custom column to parse it properly:

powerquery

= Date.FromText(
    Text.PadStart(Text.From([Month]), 2, "0") & "-" &
    Text.PadStart(Text.From([Day]), 2, "0") & "-" &
    Text.From([Year]),
    "en-US"
)
Or even better (explicit with locale):

powerquery

= Date.FromText(
    Text.PadStart(Text.From([Month]), 2, "0") & "-" &
    Text.PadStart(Text.From([Day]), 2, "0") & "-" &
    Text.From([Year])
, [Format="en-US"])
☑ Why this works: en-US culture understands MM-DD-YYYY format.

🔥 Alternative: Use #date() directly (No parsing needed!)
powerquery

= #date([Year], [Month], [Day])
➡ This avoids building a text string, and directly constructs a date from parts — safest and most recommended.

📌 Final Recommendation:
If your source has Month, Day, and Year in separate columns, just do:

powerquery

= #date([Year], [Month], [Day])
→ This never fails unless the date is invalid (like Feb 30).
